---
// The popup shell (PK-69) — one `<dialog>`, built once so that the five surfaces design
// feedback asks for a popup on (add item, edit item, create pack, edit pack details,
// add-to-pack) can land in any order without five components drifting apart on focus,
// dismissal, mobile layout or what happens when the script never arrives.
//
// THIS TICKET SHIPS NO PAGE CHANGES. Nothing renders this component yet; PK-71/72/74 do.
// What exists here is the shell and the decisions behind it, and the decisions are in
// `src/lib/modal.ts` rather than in the `<script>` block at the foot of this file — see that
// module's header for why a `<script>` block is code nothing in this repository can execute.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// IT DEPARTS FROM THE WRITTEN DESIGN LANGUAGE, AND SAYS SO
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `docs/design/DESIGN.md` §13 refuses "two overlapping papers": "nothing lies on a sheet and
// nothing hangs off one." A modal is exactly that — a sheet lying on top of the page, with
// the page dimmed behind it. This component knowingly departs from the language on the
// strength of the design feedback that asks for popups in five places, and it is written
// down here rather than quietly done, because §13's other refusals (tilt, tape, boxed cards
// inside cards, borders as separators) are still in force and this is not a licence to
// revisit them.
//
// DESIGN.md IS NOT UPDATED BY THIS TICKET. A sibling ticket owns that file, and a second
// edit to it here is a merge conflict that breaks whichever of the two lands second. When
// the language is amended, this paragraph is what the amendment should be arguing with.
//
// What the shell does keep is everything §13 is actually protecting. The sheet is `--note`
// on a `--shadow-ink` scrim, never black (tokens.css: "a black shadow on warm paper reads as
// a hole"). It is square, because sheets are square (§6 puts the 6px radius on controls, not
// on surfaces). It does not animate, because paper does not (§11) — which is also why there
// is no `prefers-reduced-motion` block below: `global.css` already collapses CSS transitions
// globally, and there is no JS-driven motion here to escape it.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// FOR FORMS. NOT FOR CONFIRMING A DELETE.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This codebase has a deliberate, repeatedly-recorded rule against client-side confirmation
// dialogs in front of destructive writes — `src/lib/account-deletion.ts:22`,
// `src/pages/gear/index.astro:154` and `src/pages/account/index.astro:54` each state it, and
// each replaces one with a two-step server flow where the first submission writes nothing.
// A popup shell is the obvious thing to reach for the next time someone wants "are you
// sure?", and reaching for it would undo all three. The rule is not about `window.confirm()`
// specifically; it is that a destructive step must be something a person performed on
// purpose against a page the server rendered, not something a script drew over the one they
// were already on.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// HOW A CONSUMER WIRES ONE UP
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   <a href="/gear/new" data-modal-open="add-item" class="btn btn-primary">Add item</a>
//
//   <Modal id="add-item" open={Astro.url.pathname === '/gear/new'}>
//     <Fragment slot="title">Add an item</Fragment>
//     <form method="POST" id="add-item-form"> ... </form>
//     <Fragment slot="actions">
//       <a href="/gear" class="btn btn-quiet">Cancel</a>
//       <button type="submit" form="add-item-form" class="btn btn-primary">Save</button>
//     </Fragment>
//   </Modal>
//
// THE TRIGGER IS A REAL LINK TO A REAL PAGE and stays one if nothing below ever runs — a
// 404'd bundle, a CSP refusal, an extension, an exception thrown earlier in the same bundle.
// `upgradeTrigger` cancels the navigation only once the dialog is genuinely up, and refuses
// to upgrade anything that is not an `<a href>` for exactly that reason. This is the
// inversion `src/pages/gear/index.astro:710-720` sets out: render the working thing
// unconditionally, upgrade it on the success path, and never use `<noscript>`, which guards
// scripting being ENABLED rather than this script having RUN.
//
// THE FORM LIVES IN THE DEFAULT SLOT AND THE SUBMIT BUTTON IN `actions` REFERS TO IT BY
// `form="…"`. A slot cannot span two slots, and putting the `<form>` around the whole shell
// would put the shell's own markup inside every consumer's form. HTML's `form` attribute is
// the join, and it submits with scripting off exactly as a nested button would.
//
// `open` RENDERS `<dialog open>`, which the browser shows IN FLOW — no top layer, no
// backdrop. That is the point: a direct visit to `/gear/new` shows a working form on a
// working page with no JavaScript at all, and the script promotes it into the top layer if
// and only if it can. `openFrom()` in `src/lib/modal.ts` puts the attribute back if the
// promotion throws.
//
// A CONSUMER THAT NEEDS THE CONTROLLER — to close its own dialog after a save, which PK-71,
// 72 and 74 all do — calls `initModals(document)` again and reads the returned
// `ReadonlyMap<string, ModalController>` by `id`. A second pass is additive: an already-wired
// dialog hands back the SAME controller, and any trigger rendered since (by a hydrated
// island, say) is upgraded on the way through. There is no other supported handle, and
// `ModalController` deliberately does not expose the `<dialog>` element itself.
//
// THE THREE `data-` ATTRIBUTE NAMES BELOW ARE ALSO DECLARED IN `src/lib/modal.ts`, as
// `MODAL_ATTRIBUTE`, `OPEN_ON_LOAD_ATTRIBUTE` and `TRIGGER_ATTRIBUTE`. They are written as
// literals here rather than interpolated from the imports, because `{...{ [MODAL_ATTRIBUTE]:
// '' }}` in markup costs more legibility than the join is worth — and because a consumer
// types `data-modal-open="…"` by hand in their own page, so the string has to be readable
// somewhere. `tests/modal-component.test.ts` renders this component and asserts the markup
// against those exported constants, which is what stops the two sides drifting: rename
// either and the render test fails instead of the feature silently going dead.

interface Props {
  /** The dialog's `id`. A trigger names it in `data-modal-open`, so it has to be stable and
   *  unique in the document — `initModals` refuses a second dialog claiming the same one,
   *  and refuses an empty one outright. */
  id: string;
  /** An accessible name, for the case where the `title` slot's text is not a good one (an
   *  icon, or a title carrying markup a screen reader would read badly). When omitted the
   *  dialog is labelled by the rendered heading, which is the case to prefer: a visible
   *  label and an announced one that cannot drift apart. */
  label?: string;
  /** Render the dialog already open, so a direct URL visit lands on the same surface a
   *  click would have produced. See the header — this is a real `<dialog open>` in the page
   *  flow, not a promise the script has to keep. */
  open?: boolean;
  /** Extra classes on the dialog, for a consumer that needs a wider or narrower sheet. */
  class?: string;
}

const { id, label, open = false, class: className } = Astro.props;

const titleId = `${id}-title`;
const hasTitle = Astro.slots.has('title');
const hasActions = Astro.slots.has('actions');

// A BUILD ERROR, NOT A RUNTIME ONE, AND NOT A SILENT PASS. A dialog with neither a `label`
// nor a `title` slot has no accessible name at all: a screen reader announces "dialog" and
// nothing else, which is a WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value, Level A) failure that no visual
// review can see. This frontmatter runs on the server during `astro build`, so throwing here
// fails the build of any page that renders one — which is the only feedback loop fast enough
// to matter for a mistake whose symptom is silence.
if (label === undefined && !hasTitle) {
  throw new Error(
    `<Modal id="${id}"> has neither a \`label\` prop nor a \`title\` slot, so it would ship ` +
      `with no accessible name (WCAG 4.1.2). Give it a <Fragment slot="title">, or pass ` +
      `label="…" when the visible title is not a usable name.`,
  );
}

// One or the other, never both — and the reason is the opposite of the one this comment used
// to give. Accname 1.2 §4.3.2 evaluates `aria-labelledby` (step 2B) BEFORE `aria-label`
// (step 2C), so `aria-labelledby` WINS. Emitting both would therefore not "announce
// something other than the heading": it would silently DISCARD the `label` prop a consumer
// passed precisely because the heading was not a usable name, and leave them with no
// indication their prop did nothing. So `label` suppresses the `labelledby` instead.
const labelledBy = label === undefined && hasTitle ? titleId : undefined;
---

<dialog
  id={id}
  data-modal
  data-modal-open-on-load={open ? '' : undefined}
  open={open}
  tabindex="-1"
  aria-label={label}
  aria-labelledby={labelledBy}
  class:list={['modal', className]}
>
  {
    /* THE HEADER IS UNCONDITIONAL NOW, because the close button is, and the close button is
      the only dismissal affordance that exists on a phone. Below 640px the sheet is
      full-bleed (see the media query): there is no dimmed area left to click, a touch
      keyboard has no ESC key, and `actions` is an optional slot — so a consumer who did not
      supply a Cancel link shipped a modal a phone visitor could not close at all. The h2
      is still conditional; a header holding only the close control is the case where `label`
      was passed instead of a title. */
  }
  <header
    class:list={[
      'border-hairline flex items-start gap-3 border-b p-5',
      hasTitle ? 'justify-between' : 'justify-end',
    ]}
  >
    {
      hasTitle && (
        /* The app is speaking, so this is Inter — the default, deliberately not overridden
           (DESIGN.md §4). `.section-title` rather than `.page-title`: the page behind the
           dialog still owns the h1, and a modal that shouted at 34px would read as a second
           page rather than as something opened on top of one. */
        <h2 id={titleId} class="section-title">
          <slot name="title" />
        </h2>
      )
    }

    {
      /* `<form method="dialog">` RATHER THAN A CLICK HANDLER, which is what makes this a real
        control rather than a second dead one. The platform closes the dialog on submit with
        no script involved at all, and it fires `close` — so it lands on exactly the path
        `endModalSession` describes, beside ESC, the backdrop and a programmatic `close()`.
        H4's refusal of a `<button data-modal-open>` does not apply here and the difference is
        worth being exact about: THAT button is a trigger, on a page where the dialog may
        never open; THIS one only exists inside a dialog that is already up.

        `data-modal-dismiss` is what keeps it out of the initial focus. It is the first
        tabbable element in the sheet, so without the marker every consumer's modal would
        open focused on Close rather than on their first field — see `focusFirstWithin`. It
        stays in the tab order, one Tab away.

        `aria-label` because the glyph is a glyph: a screen reader announcing "✕" is
        announcing nothing. The span is `aria-hidden` so the name is the label and not the
        two concatenated. */
    }
    <form method="dialog" class="modal-dismiss shrink-0">
      <button type="submit" class="btn btn-quiet" aria-label="Close" data-modal-dismiss>
        <span aria-hidden="true">✕</span>
      </button>
    </form>
  </header>

  {
    /* THE ONLY SCROLLING BOX. `min-h-0` is what makes it work: a flex child's default
      `min-height: auto` refuses to shrink below its content, so without it the body grows to
      fit the form and pushes the actions off the bottom of a 375px screen — which is
      precisely the failure the mobile requirement names. `overscroll-contain` stops a scroll
      that reaches the end of this box from chaining to the page behind it. */
  }
  <div class="min-h-0 flex-1 overflow-y-auto overscroll-contain p-5">
    <slot />
  </div>

  {
    hasActions && (
      <footer class="modal-actions border-hairline flex flex-wrap items-center justify-end gap-3 border-t px-5 pt-5">
        <slot name="actions" />
      </footer>
    )
  }
</dialog>

{
  /* THE ONLY <style> BLOCK IN ANY .astro FILE IN THIS REPOSITORY, and the exception is
    argued rather than assumed. Styling here is Tailwind utilities plus paper.css's
    `@layer components` vocabulary, and everything above that a utility can express is
    written as one — the flex column, the scrolling body, the hairline dividers, the
    padding. What is left is the part utilities genuinely cannot reach:

      1. `::backdrop` is a pseudo-element of an element in the TOP LAYER, and Tailwind 4's
         `backdrop:` variant cannot state a rule about one that has no element to hang a
         class on. (What this used to say — that the utility would have to "out-argue the
         UA's own `dialog::backdrop` rule" — was wrong, and wrong in the direction this file
         gets right two paragraphs down: an author declaration beats a UA one whatever the
         specificity. The real objection is plainer: the variant emits into the utilities
         layer against a class list on the dialog, and one plain rule on the component says
         what it means in one line.)
      2. The `<dialog>` element's UA box — its `position`, `inset`, `margin: auto` centring
         and `max-width` — differs by state (`[open]` for the no-JS fallback and `:modal`
         for the presented one), and a class list cannot vary with a pseudo-class the server
         has no way to know.
      3. `env(safe-area-inset-bottom)` inside a `max()`. Expressible as an arbitrary value,
         at the cost of a class string no reviewer can read.

    paper.css is NOT touched, and the reason is stronger than it was written as: `.sheet` is
    almost exactly this surface, and it sets `position: relative` — which, being an AUTHOR
    declaration, would WIN over the UA's `position: fixed` and drop a presented modal out of
    the centred box the top layer put it in. (The previous version of this note had the UA
    winning, which would have made reuse merely lossy rather than broken.) Its two corner-curl
    pseudo-elements of §2.4 do not survive either: a curl drawn against a dimmed scrim is a
    shadow on a shadow. Sibling tickets own that file besides. */
}
<style>
  /* THE SHEET, AND THE IN-FLOW LAYOUT THAT IS ALSO THE NO-JS FALLBACK.
     Only ever `[open]`: the UA's `dialog:not([open]) { display: none }` is a UA rule, and an
     author `display: flex` beats it regardless of specificity — an unqualified selector here
     would leave every modal on the page permanently visible.

     A server-rendered `<dialog open>` is an ordinary block in the page, and it is styled as
     an ordinary sheet: the UA's `position: absolute` would otherwise pull it out of the flow
     and hang it off whatever happened to be positioned above it. The shadow is `.sheet`'s
     ambient pair rather than the lift below, because nothing has been lifted — this sheet is
     lying on the page, not over it.

     THIS USED TO BE WRITTEN AS `.modal[open]:not(:modal)`, WHICH BROKE IN EXACTLY THE
     BROWSERS THE FALLBACK EXISTS FOR. `:not()` is not forgiving, so a whole rule whose
     selector mentions `:modal` is DISCARDED by any engine that does not know that
     pseudo-class — and there is a real population of them that nonetheless supports
     `<dialog>`: `:modal` landed in Chrome 105, Firefox 103 and Safari 15.6, while
     `showModal()` had been there since Chrome 37, Firefox 98 and Safari 15.4. Those engines
     dropped the fallback layout and rendered the no-JS form out of the flow. Stating the
     fallback UNCONDITIONALLY and letting `.modal:modal` below override it inverts the
     failure: an engine that does not know `:modal` now drops the PRESENTED rule instead and
     falls back to a readable in-flow sheet, which is the direction a fallback is supposed to
     fail in.

     The reviewed remedy was `@supports selector(:modal)` around the `:not()` version. It
     would work — `@supports selector()` predates `<dialog>` everywhere that matters (Chrome
     83, Firefox 69, Safari 14.1) — but it costs a second copy of these five declarations in
     the `@supports not` branch, and two copies of a fallback is how the two stop matching.
     Overriding needs no copy. */
  .modal[open] {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    padding: 0;
    border: 1px solid rgba(var(--shadow-ink), 0.08);
    background-color: var(--note);
    color: var(--ink);
    /* Square, like every other surface in this language: §6 puts the 6px radius on controls,
       and sheets do not have one. */
    border-radius: 0;
    /* Clipped so the head and the footer cannot be scrolled away — only the body scrolls. */
    overflow: hidden;
    position: static;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 100%;
    margin: 0;
    box-shadow:
      0 1px 2px rgba(var(--shadow-ink), 0.08),
      0 6px 18px -6px rgba(var(--shadow-ink), 0.1);
  }

  /* PRESENTED. `:modal` rather than a class the script sets, because it is the platform's own
     answer to "is this actually in the top layer" and so cannot disagree with reality. It
     comes after the block above and carries the same specificity, so it wins on order —
     every declaration it needs to overturn is restated here rather than left to chance.

     `--shadow-drag` is the site's one LIFT shadow (tokens.css: "the only shadow that says
     something has been picked up off the page"), which is the honest description of a modal
     and the closest precedent the token sheet has. `100%` rather than `100vw`: for a fixed
     box with `inset: 0` the percentage resolves against the viewport minus any classic
     scrollbar, where `100vw` includes it and pushes the sheet sideways. */
  .modal:modal {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    margin: auto;
    width: min(560px, calc(100% - 2 * var(--cell)));
    max-width: calc(100% - 2 * var(--cell));
    max-height: calc(100dvh - 2 * var(--cell));
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-drag);
  }

  /* THE CLOSE BUTTON IS SHOWN ONLY WHILE THE DIALOG IS ACTUALLY PRESENTED, and hidden by
     default rather than the other way round. In the no-JS fallback the dialog IS the page a
     visitor navigated to; a control that removed the `open` attribute would delete the form
     they asked for and leave them on a blank surface with only the Back button — the exact
     failure the fallback exists to prevent, added by the affordance meant to help. Hiding by
     default is also the safe side of the `:not()` problem above: an engine that does not know
     `:modal` drops this rule and keeps the button hidden, where the inverse would have shown
     it on the fallback. Such an engine loses the affordance and keeps ESC and the backdrop. */
  .modal-dismiss {
    display: none;
  }

  .modal:modal .modal-dismiss {
    display: block;
  }

  /* The scrim. Composed from `--shadow-ink` rather than from black, for the reason
     tokens.css gives in capitals — "a black shadow on warm paper reads as a hole" — and
     composed rather than tokenised because there is no scrim token and one value used once is
     not a scale. .45 sinks the page far enough that the sheet reads as the only live surface
     while leaving the page recognisable behind it.

     THE `var()` HERE DEPENDS ON `::backdrop` INHERITING FROM ITS ORIGINATING ELEMENT, which
     is worth writing down because it was not always true: `::backdrop` used to have no parent
     at all, and inheritance landed only in Chrome 122, Firefox 120 and Safari 17.4. Below
     those, `--shadow-ink` resolves to nothing, the declaration is invalid at computed-value
     time, and the scrim is simply absent. The alternative is a hard `rgba(60, 45, 20, …)` at
     this call site, which CONTRIBUTING.md and tokens.css both refuse outright — a colour that
     is not in the token sheet belongs in DESIGN.md, and a second copy of one that is there is
     how two values drift apart. So the token wins and the browser floor moves; the dialog is
     still fully usable without the scrim, which is what makes that the cheaper of the two
     failures. VERIFIED, not assumed: on Chrome 151 the presented dialog's `::backdrop`
     computes to `rgba(60, 45, 20, 0.45)`, i.e. the inheritance really does reach it. */
  .modal::backdrop {
    background-color: rgba(var(--shadow-ink), 0.45);
  }

  /* MOBILE IS THE FIRST-CLASS CASE, not a narrowed desktop one. At 375px the sheet is the
     whole viewport: full height, no gutters, no border, with the body scrolling inside it
     and the actions pinned below it by the flex column above. Nothing is centred, because a
     centred card at this width is a card with 20px of dimmed page on either side and less
     room for the form than the keyboard is about to take away. It is also why the header
     carries a close button: at this width there is no backdrop left to click. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .modal:modal {
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 100%;
      height: 100dvh;
      max-height: 100dvh;
      margin: 0;
      border-width: 0;
    }
  }

  /* The actions row is the one thing that can end up under a home indicator or a rounded
     corner, because it is the only thing flush with the bottom edge on a full-height sheet.
     `max()` keeps the 20px cell where there is no inset to respect, which is every desktop
     and most phones. */
  .modal-actions {
    padding-bottom: max(var(--cell), env(safe-area-inset-bottom));
  }
</style>

{
  /* A bundled module rather than `is:inline`, which is the opposite of the choice
    `src/pages/gear/index.astro:1041` makes and for a reason that inverts cleanly. That
    script had to run during parsing and had to be immune to a bundle that never arrives,
    because its job was to REMOVE a working control. This one only ever ADDS behaviour: if
    the module never loads, every trigger is still a link and every open-on-load dialog is
    still a rendered form. Nothing is taken away, so nothing is lost by waiting for the
    bundle — and being a module is what lets the decisions live in src/lib/modal.ts under
    test instead of being retyped here where nothing can reach them.

    THE RETURN VALUE IS DISCARDED HERE AND THAT IS NOT A LOSS. `initModals` is idempotent and
    additive, so a consumer that wants the controller for its own dialog calls it again and
    reads the map — see the header. Holding the map in this block instead would mean putting
    it somewhere a consumer's own script could find it, i.e. on `window`, which is a global
    this repository does not have and should not acquire for the sake of saving one call. */
}
<script>
  import { initModals } from '../lib/modal';

  initModals(document);
</script>
